#
# Local Docker overrides.
#
# Copy this file to `.env` (which is gitignored) and uncomment what you need.
# `docker compose` reads `.env` from the project root automatically, so no
# tracked file has to be modified -- and nothing shows up in `git status`.
#
# Every value below defaults to what `docker-compose.yaml` used to hardcode,
# so leaving `.env` absent keeps the out-of-the-box behaviour unchanged.
#

#
# Host interface the containers bind to. Unset by default, which binds every
# interface over both IPv4 and IPv6.
#
# This is the recommended way to free up ports 80/443 for another local dev
# environment: point this stack at a loopback alias instead of moving it off
# the standard ports, so site URLs stay portless and nothing else breaks.
#
# See "Changing the host ports" in `.docker/readme.md` for the loopback-alias
# and hosts-file steps that go with it.
#
#WORDCAMP_BIND_IP=127.0.0.2

#
# Host ports.
#
# `WORDCAMP_MAILCATCHER_PORT` and `WORDCAMP_DB_PORT` are safe to change freely.
#
# `WORDCAMP_HTTP_PORT` and `WORDCAMP_HTTPS_PORT` are NOT. This stack assumes
# portless hostnames in several places (the `HTTP_HOST` switch in
# `.docker/wp-config.php`, nginx's HTTP->HTTPS redirect, the site URLs seeded
# into the database, and the redirects in `public_html/wp-content/sunrise*.php`).
# Setting them doesn't merely route to the wrong network -- with
# WORDCAMP_HTTPS_PORT=8443 every URL 302s to a portless
# `https://central.wordcamp.test/`, which is now a dead port. Use
# `WORDCAMP_BIND_IP` above instead.
#
#WORDCAMP_HTTP_PORT=80
#WORDCAMP_HTTPS_PORT=443
#WORDCAMP_MAILCATCHER_PORT=1080
#WORDCAMP_DB_PORT=3307
